# Citation File Format 1.0.0 — https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# CC0 attaches no conditions to the data package, and MIT asks only that the notice be
# kept. Citing this work is therefore a request, not a requirement. This file is here so
# that the request is easy to honour.
#
# The author list was completed on 2026-09-06 and now matches the manuscript, in the same
# order. The version below is the one the git tag v1.0.0 marks and the one Zenodo
# archives; if the tag moves, this moves with it, or the archived snapshot contradicts
# its own metadata. TWO THINGS ARE STILL OPEN, and are deliberately absent rather than
# guessed:
#   * one ORCID, for E. Urazimbetova, which has not been supplied;
#   * `preferred-citation`, which is added once the paper has a DOI. Until then there is
#     no article to point at, and inventing one would be worse than pointing at nothing.
#
# `doi` below is the VERSIONED DOI of v1.0.0 -- the snapshot the paper cites -- and
# not the concept DOI 10.5281/zenodo.22549213, which follows the latest version and
# is in README.md instead. CFF 1.0.x has room for one DOI, and the paper names a
# version, so the versioned one is the one that belongs here.
#
# It was minted FROM the v1.0.0 tag and so arrived after it. The tag is deliberately
# not moved: the manuscript cites commit 197dcf5 by name and Zenodo archived that
# commit. The archived copy of this file therefore has no `doi` key and this one
# does, which is what archiving a release always looks like.

cff-version: 1.0.0
message: "If this software or data package is useful to you, please cite it as below."
title: "GateMPC: identifying a canal gate law from an open archive, and what it costs predictive control"
abstract: >-
  At a gated canal structure the published continuous discharge is not a measurement:
  it is the output of a rating equation, so checking a gate law against that series is
  a tautology that returns a near-perfect fit. This repository holds the detector for
  that circularity, its confirmation at a second structure whose rating takes a
  different form, the non-circular check against independent field gaugings, and a
  closed-loop measurement of what a controller loses by carrying the benchmark's gate
  law. It ships the normalised USGS data package the results are computed from, the
  scripts that compute them, the tests, and a desktop application that shows the
  results and applies the method to a structure of the reader's own.
type: software
